covetousness, wickedness, deceit, lasciviousness, an evil eye, blasphemy, pride, foolishness:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;all these evil things come from within, and defile the man.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ™And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙmFor a certain woman, whose young daughter had an unclean spirit, heard of him, and came and fell at his feet:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~the woman was a Greek, a Syrophenician by nation; and she besought him that he would cast forth the devil out of her daughter.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰But Jesus said unto her, Let the children first be filled: for it is not meet to take the children's bread, and to cast it unto the dogs.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiAnd she answered and said unto him, Yes, Lord: yet the dogs under the table eat of the children's crumbs.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXAnd he said unto her, For this saying go thy way; the devil is gone out of thy daughter.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeAnd when she was come to her house, she found the devil gone out, and her daughter laid upon the bed.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse%¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ†And again, departing from the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, he came unto the sea of Galilee, through the midst of the coasts of Decapolis.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~And they bring unto him one that was deaf, and had an impediment in his speech; and they beseech him to put his hand upon him.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqAnd he took him aside from the multitude, and put his fingers into his ears, and he spit, and touched his tongue;¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWand looking up to heaven, he sighed, and saith unto him, Ephphatha, that is, Be opened.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbAnd straightway his ears were opened, and the string of his tongue was loosed, and he spake plain.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ€And he charged them that they should tell no man: but the more he charged them, so much the more a great deal they published it;¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙand were beyond measure astonished, saying, He hath done all things well: he maketh both the deaf to hear, and the dumb to speak.¦layout ªlast_verse%